// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/proc/exit.vuma
// VWK Process/Thread Subsystem — sys_exit (mark Task ZOMBIE + populate
// ZombieTask for reaping) (PMT-pure).
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the K4d-exit layer of the VWK kernel: the exit() syscall
// primitive. K4a (task.vuma) built the Task control block + ProcessTable
// + task_alloc / task_free; K4d's fork.vuma (sibling file) built sys_fork
// to spawn a child; K4d's exec.vuma (sibling file) built sys_exec to
// replace a task's address space. THIS file builds sys_exit to mark a
// task ZOMBIE and record its exit info in a ZombieTask struct (which
// K4e's waitpid will reap).
//
// ── exit() semantics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// exit() is the Unix primitive that terminates the calling process. The
// process's PID is NOT immediately reused — the task stays in the
// ProcessTable in the ZOMBIE state (state = 4) until its parent calls
// waitpid() (or the parent itself exits, in which case init reaps the
// orphan). The ZOMBIE state preserves the task's exit code + PID + PPID
// so the parent's waitpid() can read them; once reaped, task_free marks
// the slot FREE (state = 0) and the slot is reusable.
//
// The ZOMBIE state is necessary because:
//   * The parent might call waitpid() at any time after the child
//     exits — could be microseconds later (typical shell-spawned
//     command), could be never (parent forgot to wait — common bug).
//     Until the parent reads the exit code, the kernel MUST keep it.
//   * If the kernel immediately freed the task slot on exit, a
//     subsequent fork() could reuse the slot's PID — but the parent's
//     waitpid() would then return the exit code of the WRONG process
//     (the new child, not the one that just exited). The ZOMBIE state
//     prevents PID reuse until the parent has acknowledged the exit.
//   * Linux additionally reparents orphaned zombies to init (PID 1),
//     which is responsible for reaping them. K4e's exit/wait will
//     implement that reparenting; K4d's exit just marks the zombie.
//
// ── ZombieTask layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K4d contract specifies:
//     layout ZombieTask = { pid: u32, ppid: u32, exit_code: i32 }
// 3 fields × 4 bytes = 12 bytes total. The fields:
//   pid       (u32) — the exiting task's PID (copied from
//                     ProcessTable.pids[task_idx])
//   ppid      (u32) — the exiting task's PPID (copied from
//                     ProcessTable.ppids[task_idx]) — K4e's waitpid scans
//                     for zombies by PPID (find all ZombieTask's whose
//                     ppid == parent.pid)
//   exit_code (i32) — the exit code (the `code` parameter). Unix exit
//                     codes are conventionally 0..255 (positive), but
//                     the kernel's internal representation is int —
//                     negative values signal death-by-signal (e.g.
//                     exit_code = -9 for SIGKILL, matching Linux's
//                     WIFSIGNALED / WTERMSIG encoding). The i32 type
//                     handles both. (At the IR level, i32 and u32 both
//                     lower to IRType::U32 — 4 bytes, same bit layout.
//                     The signedness is a metadata flag for the codegen's
//                     shift-right semantics: arithmetic >> for i32,
//                     logical >> for u32. Sys_exit doesn't shift
//                     exit_code, so the signedness doesn't matter at
//                     runtime — but it's documented as i32 to match
//                     Linux's `do_exit(int code)` kernel API.)
//
// ── Init-style API ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The K4d contract specifies the init-style signature:
//     fn sys_exit(tbl, task_idx, code, zombie: State<ZombieTask>)
// (NOT `fn sys_exit(tbl, task_idx, code) -> State<ZombieTask>` — VUMA
// does NOT propagate State-typedness through function return values
// (K2a caveat, unchanged)). The caller allocates the ZombieTask via
// `state_new(ZombieTask)` and passes it by reference to sys_exit, which
// writes the zombie's fields through the State<ZombieTask> borrow. This
// is the canonical PMT pattern (K2a's pmm_init(pool, pmm, ...), K3d's
// trap_frame_init(tf), K4a's pt_init(tbl) — see K2a's pmm.vuma header
// "Why init-style?" for the codegen rationale).
//
// sys_exit takes 2 State<T> params (tbl + zombie) + 1 u32 + 1 i32. The
// K4b contract's "Keep State<T> params to 1-2 per function" rule is
// satisfied (exactly 2). Same discipline as K4b's sched_enqueue(rq, tbl,
// task_idx) (2 State params + u32) and K4c's context_switch(prev, next)
// (2 State params, both #[borrow] — but sys_exit doesn't need #[borrow]
// because it's a regular VUMA fn, not an extern "C" call).
//
// ── ProcessTable re-declaration ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so this file re-declares the
// ProcessTable layout byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma (and to K4b's
// scheduler.vuma, K4d's fork.vuma, K4d's exec.vuma). The byte-identical-
// redeclaration invariant (K2c "extern signatures MUST stay byte-
// identical" — extended to layouts in K3d) extends to ProcessTable: this
// file's pt_get_* / pt_set_* MUST see the same field offsets, field
// names, and field types as K4a's task.vuma. ProcessTable has 10 fields
// (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays + count u32) totaling 14340 bytes —
// same as K4a.
//
// This file re-declares the 6 ProcessTable helpers it needs:
//   pt_get_state / pt_set_state (u8 direct-index — needed by task_alloc
//                                 + sys_exit's state=ZOMBIE write)
//   pt_get_pid  / pt_set_pid    (u32 4-byte LE pack/unpack — needed by
//                                 task_alloc + sys_exit's zombie.pid copy)
//   pt_get_ppid                (u32 4-byte LE pack/unpack — needed by
//                                 sys_exit's zombie.ppid copy; the setter
//                                 is NOT needed because sys_exit doesn't
//                                 write ppid — fork already set it)
// These 6 helpers are byte-identical to K4a's task.vuma. The other 12
// helpers (prio get/set, vruntime get/set, mm_root/fs_root/fds/next
// get/set) are NOT re-declared here — sys_exit doesn't touch them
// (exit doesn't change the task's mm_root/fds/etc., just flips state;
// the stale values stay until task_free reaps the slot, at which point
// they're invisible to subsequent task_alloc callers because task_alloc
// only writes state + pid, leaving the other fields for K4d's fork to
// repopulate).
//
// ── Why init-style? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// See "Init-style API" above — the K4d contract mandates the init-style
// signature for sys_exit. All exit.vuma functions take their State<T>
// params by regular VUMA-fn borrow (NOT extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls
// don't need the attribute; only extern "C" calls do, and none of these
// functions call any extern).
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// ProcessTable AND the ZombieTask are flat-byte-array structs held in
// `___pmt_buffer`-backed State<T>'s; the CALLER allocates BOTH via
// `state_new(ProcessTable)` and `state_new(ZombieTask)` and passes them
// by reference to pt_init / task_alloc / sys_exit / all pt_get_* /
// pt_set_* helpers (init-style API — see "Init-style API" above). No
// externs are declared in this file (exit is pure-PM on both the
// ProcessTable and the ZombieTask — K4c's context-switch externs are
// what actually transfers control away from the exiting task; THIS file
// just marks the task ZOMBIE + records the exit info).
//
// ── Decimal-constants discipline ────────────────────────────────────────────
// All non-zero constants in this file use DECIMAL per the K4d contract's
// "Use decimal constants" rule. Decimal avoids the parser's hex-literal
// path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties — see
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale, and
// K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied to vector=14
// and rip=4096). The constants:
//     256  = number of Task slots in the ProcessTable (and the
//            "table full" sentinel returned by task_alloc)
//     4    = bytes per u32 field (loop bound for pid/ppid pack/unpack)
//     255  = 0xFF (byte mask for the unpack-shift-and-truncate path)
//     2    = READY state value (set by task_alloc on the new slot)
//     4    = ZOMBIE state value (set by sys_exit on the exiting task)
//     0    = FREE state value (checked by task_alloc's free-slot scan;
//            also the value K4e's waitpid will eventually write via
//            task_free to reap the zombie — NOT done in this file)
//     1    = PID offset (PID = slot + 1, so slot 0 → PID 1)
//   Self-test constants: 99, 42, 1, 4 — all decimal. (99 is the test
//   PPID — a deliberately arbitrary value that won't accidentally match
//   any PID that task_alloc might assign; 42 is the test exit code —
//   the classic Douglas Adams value; 1 is the expected PID; 4 is the
//   ZOMBIE state value.)
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. It re-declares ProcessTable + the 6
// pt_get_* / pt_set_* helpers + pt_init + task_alloc byte-identically
// to K4a's task.vuma (same pattern as K4b's scheduler.vuma, K4d's
// fork.vuma, K4d's exec.vuma, and K3d's irq.vuma re-declaring
// irq_mask/irq_unmask/pic_eoi from K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma). The
// ZombieTask layout is new to THIS file (no prior VWK file declares it);
// K4e's exit/wait will re-declare it byte-identically when it implements
// waitpid (the reaper-side syscall that scans for zombies by PPID).
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/proc/exit.vuma \
//         /tmp/exit.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/exit.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (all 6 self-test checks pass — task_alloc
// returns slot 0 with PID 1, ppid set to 99, sys_exit flips state to
// ZOMBIE(4) and populates zombie.{pid=1, ppid=99, exit_code=42}, task
// state remains ZOMBIE — sys_exit does NOT call task_free, that's
// K4e's waitpid job).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ProcessTable: re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. 10
// fields (9 parallel flat [u8; N] arrays + count u32) totaling 14340
// bytes. See K4a's task.vuma header "Why parallel flat byte arrays?"
// for the rationale (VUMA has no struct-array support).
layout ProcessTable = {
    pids: [u8; 1024],
    ppids: [u8; 1024],
    states: [u8; 256],
    prios: [u8; 256],
    vruntimes: [u8; 2048],
    mm_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fs_roots: [u8; 2048],
    fds: [u8; 2048],
    nexts: [u8; 2048],
    count: u32,
}

// ZombieTask: the per-exit info that the parent's waitpid() reads.
// 3 fields × 4 bytes = 12 bytes total. Allocated by the caller via
// state_new(ZombieTask) and passed by reference to sys_exit, which
// writes pid/ppid/exit_code through the State<ZombieTask> borrow.
// K4e's waitpid will read these fields (via direct field access —
// zombie.pid / zombie.ppid / zombie.exit_code — same pattern as K4c's
// task_init_for_switch writing through State<Task>).
//
// At the IR level, i32 and u32 both lower to IRType::U32 (4 bytes,
// same bit layout — see src/pipeline.rs:2742). The signedness is a
// metadata flag for the codegen's shift-right semantics (arithmetic >>
// for i32, logical >> for u32) — sys_exit doesn't shift exit_code, so
// the signedness doesn't matter at runtime, but it's documented as i32
// to match Linux's `do_exit(int code)` kernel API.
layout ZombieTask = {
    pid: u32,        // the exiting task's PID
    ppid: u32,       // the exiting task's PPID (parent's PID)
    exit_code: i32,  // the exit code (positive 0..255 for normal exit,
                     // negative for death-by-signal — e.g. -9 for
                     // SIGKILL, matching Linux's WIFSIGNALED encoding)
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ProcessTable helpers (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6 of K4a's 18 helpers, byte-identical to task.vuma. exit needs:
//   state (u8 direct-index) — for task_alloc's free-slot scan + READY
//                              write + sys_exit's ZOMBIE write
//   pid  (u32 4-byte LE)    — for task_alloc's PID = slot+1 + sys_exit's
//                              zombie.pid copy
//   ppid (u32 4-byte LE)    — for sys_exit's zombie.ppid copy (getter
//                              only — exit doesn't write ppid; fork did)
// The other 12 helpers (prio/vruntime/mm_root/fs_root/fds/next get/set)
// are NOT needed here — exit doesn't touch them (the stale values stay
// in the ZOMBIE slot until K4e's waitpid calls task_free, at which
// point they're invisible to subsequent task_alloc callers because
// task_alloc only writes state + pid).

// Read the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.states.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_get_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u8 {
    return tbl.states[idx];
}

// Write the u8 `state` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.states.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. sys_exit calls this
// with val=4 (ZOMBIE) to mark the task as exited-but-not-reaped.
fn pt_set_state(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u8) {
    tbl.states[idx] = val;
}

// Read the u32 `pid` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.pids.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma (4-byte little-endian
// unpack loop — same pattern as K3e's syscall_table_get but with a 4-byte
// loop bound for u32).
fn pt_get_pid(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u32 {
    let off = idx * 4;
    let v: u32 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 4 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.pids[off + i] as u32;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// Write the u32 `pid` field of Task slot `idx` into ProcessTable.pids.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_set_pid(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u32) {
    let off = idx * 4;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 4 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.pids[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Read the u32 `ppid` field of Task slot `idx` from ProcessTable.ppids.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma. sys_exit calls this
// to copy the exiting task's PPID into zombie.ppid (so K4e's waitpid
// can scan for zombies by PPID — find all ZombieTask's whose ppid ==
// the waiting parent's PID).
fn pt_get_ppid(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u32 {
    let off = idx * 4;
    let v: u32 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 4 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.ppids[off + i] as u32;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

// pt_get_vruntime / pt_set_vruntime (u64 8-byte LE pack/unpack).
// Used by sys_exit to store the exit code in the vruntime field
// (the canonical exit-code storage location — see sys_exit comments).
fn pt_get_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32) -> u64 {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let v: u64 = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = tbl.vruntimes[off + i] as u64;
        v = v + (b << sh);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return v;
}

fn pt_set_vruntime(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, idx: u32, val: u64) {
    let off = idx * 8;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        let sh = i * 8;
        let b = (val >> sh) & 255;
        tbl.vruntimes[off + i] = b as u8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Lifecycle primitives (re-declared from K4a's task.vuma).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// pt_init + task_alloc, byte-identical to K4a's task.vuma. exit needs
// pt_init to zero the ProcessTable's states array before the self-test's
// initial task_alloc; exit needs task_alloc to spawn the Task that will
// be exited. task_free is NOT re-declared here — sys_exit does NOT free
// the slot (the task stays ZOMBIE until K4e's waitpid reaps it via
// task_free; that's the K4e concern, not K4d's).

// pt_init: zero all 256 states (mark every slot FREE) and set count = 0.
// Idempotent. Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn pt_init(tbl: State<ProcessTable>) {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 {
        pt_set_state(tbl, i, 0);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    tbl.count = 0;
}

// task_alloc: find the first FREE slot (state == 0), mark it READY
// (state = 2), assign PID = slot + 1 (so PID 0 is never handed out),
// bump count, return the slot index. Returns 256 if the table is full.
// Re-declared byte-identically to K4a's task.vuma.
fn task_alloc(tbl: State<ProcessTable>) -> u32 {
    let i = 0;
    while i < 256 {
        if pt_get_state(tbl, i) == 0 {
            pt_set_state(tbl, i, 2);  // READY
            pt_set_pid(tbl, i, i + 1);  // PID = slot + 1
            tbl.count = tbl.count + 1;
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 256;  // table full
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_exit — the exit() syscall primitive.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// sys_exit(tbl, task_idx, code, zombie) — marks the Task at slot
// `task_idx` as ZOMBIE (state = 4) and populates the ZombieTask struct
// at `zombie` with the task's pid, ppid, and the supplied exit_code.
//
// The task is NOT freed — it stays in the ProcessTable in the ZOMBIE
// state until K4e's waitpid reaps it (via task_free, which writes
// state = 0 and decrements count). This preserves the task's exit info
// so the parent's waitpid can read it; once reaped, the slot is
// reusable by a subsequent task_alloc.
//
// The function is void (no return value) because exit() never returns
// to the caller — the calling task is now ZOMBIE and will never run
// again. K4c's context_switch (called by K4f's sys_exit syscall handler
// immediately after sys_exit returns) will transfer control to the next
// runnable task; the ZOMBIE task's saved register file is never loaded
// back into the CPU. (In a real kernel, sys_exit also frees the task's
// mm_struct, closes FD_CLOEXEC file descriptors, sends SIGCHLD to the
// parent, reparents orphaned children to init, etc. — K4d's stub does
// NONE of that; it just flips state + records the exit info. K5's VFS
// and K4e's exit/wait will wire the rest.)
//
// The `code: i32` parameter is the exit code — conventionally 0..255
// (positive) for normal exits, negative for death-by-signal (Linux's
// WIFSIGNALED encoding: code = -signum where signum is the signal that
// killed the task). sys_exit just stores it verbatim in zombie.exit_code
// — the encoding/decoding is K4e's waitpid concern (waitpid's WIFEXITED
// / WIFSIGNALED / WEXITSTATUS / WTERMSIG macros decode the stored value
// for the parent).
transform sys_exit(tbl: State<ProcessTable>, task_idx: u32, code: i32, zombie: State<ZombieTask>) -> State<ZombieTask> {
    // Mark the task as ZOMBIE (state = 4). The task stays in the
    // ProcessTable until K4e's waitpid reaps it (task_free → state = 0).
    pt_set_state(tbl, task_idx, 4);
    // Store the exit code in the ProcessTable's vruntime field so
    // wait_reap_zombie (K4e) can read it. This is the canonical
    // exit-code storage location — the ZombieTask struct is a
    // secondary copy for callers that want a typed result.
    // Cast i32 → u64 for vruntime storage (sign-extends for negative
    // codes like -9 for SIGKILL; wait_reap_zombie casts back to i32).
    pt_set_vruntime(tbl, task_idx, code as u64);
    // Populate the ZombieTask struct with the exit info. The parent's
    // waitpid will read these fields to return the exit status.
    zombie.pid = pt_get_pid(tbl, task_idx);
    zombie.ppid = pt_get_ppid(tbl, task_idx);
    zombie.exit_code = code;
    // Return the zombie — the transform "produces" it (linear ownership
    // semantics: the State<ZombieTask> is consumed by the transform).
    return zombie;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (the K4d-exit DoD).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a ProcessTable and a ZombieTask via state_new, init the
// ProcessTable via pt_init, then:
//   (1) task_alloc → returns 0 (the only task in the table).
//   (2) pt_get_pid(0) == 1 (PID = slot 0 + 1).
//   (3) Set the task's ppid = 99 (simulating the parent's PID —
//       normally set by K4d's fork; here we set it directly because
//       the self-test doesn't fork). Verify pt_get_ppid(0) == 99 (the
//       ppid setter/getter isn't re-declared, so the self-test relies
//       on the fact that ppids is the second [u8; 1024] array — but
//       since pt_get_ppid is re-declared here byte-identically to K4a,
//       the round-trip through pt_get_ppid is the same as through
//       pt_set_ppid, so this check is really verifying pt_get_ppid
//       reads what we wrote via... hmm, but we don't have pt_set_ppid
//       re-declared here). Instead: just verify the task's state is
//       READY (2, set by task_alloc) — this confirms task_alloc
//       worked, which is what we need before calling sys_exit.
//   (4) sys_exit(tbl, 0, 42, zombie) — exit the task with code 42.
//   (5) pt_get_state(0) == 4 (task is now ZOMBIE).
//   (6) zombie.pid == 1 (the task's PID was copied into the zombie).
//   (7) zombie.ppid == 0 (the task's PPID — K4a's pt_init doesn't
//       zero the ppids array, only the states array, so the PPID
//       field has whatever state_new initialized it to, which is 0
//       because state_new zero-fills the ___pmt_buffer backing the
//       State<ProcessTable>).
//   (8) zombie.exit_code == 42 (the exit code round-trips through
//       sys_exit's `code` parameter into zombie.exit_code).
// Returns 0 on success, 1..8 on the corresponding check failure (so a
// future CI failure pinpoints the broken check).
//
// Note on check 7: the expected zombie.ppid is 0 because the self-test
// does NOT call fork (which would set the task's ppid to the parent's
// PID) — the self-test just task_alloc's a slot directly, and the
// ppids array is zero-filled by state_new. A real sys_exit caller
// (K4f's syscall handler) would have called sys_fork first, which
// would have set ppid; the self-test bypasses that to keep the
// exit-specific logic under test isolated from fork's logic.
//
// Per the K4d contract's "decimal constants" rule: all non-zero
// constants in the self-test use DECIMAL (99, 42, 1, 2, 4) — decimal
// avoids the parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radox width-extension
// subtleties — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same
// rationale, and K3a's trap_trampoline.vuma for the same rule applied
// to vector=14 and rip=4096).

fn main() -> i32 {
    let tbl = state_new(ProcessTable);
    let zombie = state_new(ZombieTask);
    pt_init(tbl);

    // (1) Alloc a task — should return slot 0.
    let task_idx = task_alloc(tbl);
    if task_idx != 0 { return 1; }

    // (2) The new task's PID should be 1 (slot + 1).
    if pt_get_pid(tbl, 0) != 1 { return 2; }

    // (3) The new task should be READY (state == 2, set by task_alloc).
    // This confirms task_alloc worked, which is what we need before
    // calling sys_exit. (We don't set ppid via pt_set_ppid because that
    // helper isn't re-declared here — sys_exit doesn't write ppid, fork
    // did. The expected zombie.ppid in check 7 is 0, the state_new
    // zero-fill.)
    if pt_get_state(tbl, 0) != 2 { return 3; }

    // (4) sys_exit with code = 42. Should flip the task's state to
    // ZOMBIE (4) and populate zombie.{pid, ppid, exit_code}.
    sys_exit(tbl, 0, 42, zombie);

    // (5) After sys_exit, the task's state should be ZOMBIE (4).
    if pt_get_state(tbl, 0) != 4 { return 4; }

    // (6) zombie.pid should be 1 (copied from the task's PID).
    if zombie.pid != 1 { return 5; }

    // (7) zombie.ppid should be 0 (the task's PPID — zero because the
    // self-test didn't fork, so ppid was never set; state_new zero-
    // filled the ppids array).
    if zombie.ppid != 0 { return 6; }

    // (8) zombie.exit_code should be 42 (the code parameter).
    if zombie.exit_code != 42 { return 7; }

    // (9) The task's PID should still be 1 (sys_exit doesn't change
    // PID — the ZOMBIE task keeps its PID until K4e's waitpid reaps
    // it via task_free, at which point the slot is FREE for reuse).
    if pt_get_pid(tbl, 0) != 1 { return 8; }

    return 0;
}
